# ai-discoverability (Cursor)

Makes site content reachable/parseable/citable by AI crawlers and search
engines, without touching visual design.

## Steps

0. Confirm content is reachable without JS execution (`curl`/view-source,
   not devtools-rendered DOM) — most AI crawlers don't run JS reliably.
1. `robots.txt` — explicitly decide AI bot policy (GPTBot, ClaudeBot,
   PerplexityBot, Google-Extended, etc.), don't rely on wildcard
   defaults. Keep a `Sitemap:` directive.
2. `llms.txt` at site root — curated Markdown summary + key links, not an
   auto-dump of the whole sitemap.
3. Structured data (JSON-LD schema.org) matching real page content —
   validate before shipping, keep in sync with visible content.
4. Unique title/meta description per page, `rel="canonical"`, Open
   Graph/Twitter card tags, maintained `sitemap.xml` with `lastmod`.
5. Semantic HTML — one real `<h1>`, logical heading hierarchy, semantic
   elements over generic `<div>`s, descriptive link/alt text.
6. Verify with `curl -A "GPTBot"` and a structured-data validator before
   calling it done.

## Not this skill's job

- Visual/UI design — that's the project's UI/UX team/template.
- Browser-API-level implementation (Core Web Vitals, ARIA, WebMCP) — see
  `modern-web-guidance` for that.
- Guaranteeing ranking/citation — this makes a site eligible to be found,
  not first in results.
